            <ArticleTitle>Optic nerve involvement in neuro-Behcet's disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>100-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To report the ocular manifestations of neuro-Behcet's disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASES" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 34-year-old woman had recurrent meningitis. She developed diplopia, headache, and ataxia, and was diagnosed as having neuro-Behcet's disease.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBSERVATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Imaging revealed two infarct foci in the transitional midbrain and pons. After treatment with prednisolone and colchicine, the diplopia resolved. Two years later, a scotoma developed in the right eye, in which the best-corrected visual acuity was 4/200. Papillitis and a prepapillary vitreous opacity were seen in the right fundus. These findings disappeared 11 days after subconjunctival steroid injections and increased colchicine. Her vision gradually improved to 20/20 two months later.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Neuro-Behcet's disease may manifest with transient optic neuritis and prepapillary vitreous opacity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
